Task: In a fantasy game, we need different types of magical scrolls. Implement the `HealingScroll` class so it correctly inherits from the base `Scroll` class and overrides its `cast` method to provide healing-specific information.

Submitted code:
```
class Scroll {
  constructor(title, manaCost) {
    this.title = title;
    this.manaCost = manaCost;
  }

  // Define a base description that subclasses can easily append to
  get castDetails() {
    return `${this.title} is cast, costing ${this.manaCost} mana`;
  }

  cast() {
    return `${this.castDetails}.`;
  }
}

class HealingScroll extends Scroll {
  constructor(title, manaCost, healingAmount) {
    super(title, manaCost);
    this.healingAmount = healingAmount;
  }

  cast() {
    // Better Practice: Directly access properties and use the parent getter
    // No regex, no parsing, no risk of breakage if the string format changes.
    return `${this.castDetails}. It heals for ${this.healingAmount} HP.`;
  }
}

// --- Test Code ---
const fireblast = new Scroll("Fireblast", 20);
console.log(fireblast.cast());

const minorHeal = new HealingScroll("Minor Heal", 10, 50);
console.log(minorHeal.cast());

```

Does this code correctly implement the inheritance concept? Check for proper OOP usage.
